{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Shimmering Dark: Vampire Squid Grounds and the Marine Snow Recyclers",
    "date": "2026-10-15",
    "lesson": "In the oxygen minimum zone of Monterey Bay, around 600-900m, Vampyroteuthis infernalis feeds on marine snow and 'mucous marine snow' particles stirred by sinking detritus. The bacteria that colonize this falling material are specialist decomposers: protein-rich fecal pellets and gelatinous carcasses favor protease-heavy lineages, while lipid-rich fish-fall scraps favor esterases. Oxygen is nearly absent here, so grazing pressure from protists is reduced and energy budgets run lean - cells must scavenge efficiently and resist near-anoxic stress rather than outswim predators. This scenario shows how particle chemistry (not just presence) sorts the microbial community, and how low oxygen reshapes the balance between bacteria, phage and grazers.",
    "citation": "Robison, B.H. et al. Marine snow feeding ecology of Vampyroteuthis infernalis; Monterey Bay OMZ studies (MBARI)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Monterey Bay oxygen minimum zone, ~700m, Vampyroteuthis infernalis marine-snow feeding grounds"
  },
